{"id":"66db7c9b-b0a8-4a0c-a30c-3261e83ac973","object":"chat.completion","created":1785028319,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields')\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        node_dict[node['id']] = node\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if not any(n['symbol'] == sym for n in node_dict.values()):\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present')\\n    if 'root' not in node_dict:\\n        raise ValueError('Root node missing')\\n    root = node_dict['root']\\n    if root.get('removable', False):\\n        raise ValueError('Root is removable')\\n    children = {}\\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    forced_retained = set()\\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            forced_retained.add(nid)\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            forced_retained.add(nid)\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            forced_retained.add(nid)\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    candidates = [node for node in node_dict.values() if node['removable'] and node['id'] not in forced_retained]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in candidates:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\\n            if child_id in retained:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that takes a case payload with nodes and required_symbols. The task is to perform grammar-guided node reduction. Based on the description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But from the fixtures, it seems we have a tree structure with nodes having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The required_symbols list specifies symbols that must be retained in the tree after reduction. We need to decide which removable nodes to remove, while ensuring all required symbols remain represented. The objective is presumably to maximize utility or minimize something? The problem statement is vague. However, looking at the output schema: we need to return removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The candidate_artifact text says: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nSo the algorithm: We have a list of nodes. The root (id \"root\") is always retained. Nodes with symbol in required_symbols are retained (must not be removed). Nodes that are not removable (removable==false) cannot be removed. Nodes that are removable may be removed if after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., the set of symbols among retained nodes still covers required_symbols). Also, if a node has children that are retained, it cannot be removed. Because removing a parent would orphan children? Actually, \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" means that if any child of that node is retained, then the parent must be retained to keep the tree connected. But if all children are removed, then the parent may become removable? However, the algorithm processes deeper nodes first. So we should consider nodes in reverse depth order (deepest first), and within same depth, sort by node id (string) for stable tie break. For each node that is removable and not forced to be retained (like required symbol or root), we tentatively try to remove it, but only if after removal, the set of symbols of all retained nodes still includes all required_symbols. Also, we must ensure that no retained child exists for that node. Since we are processing deeper first, children will be processed before parent, so if we remove a child, it's already removed. So when considering a parent, its children's removal status is already decided. So we can check: if any child (by parent field) is still retained, then we cannot remove this parent. But careful: The parent field is a string id, we need to build parent-child relationships. Also, after we remove a node, we should not consider it in future checks.\n\nWe need to track which nodes are removed. Initially, all removable nodes are candidates. The root and nodes with symbol in required_symbols are forced retained. Also nodes that are not removable are forced retained.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n1. Parse the nodes list into a dict keyed by id. Build children list for each node (by parent field).\n2. Determine initial set of retained nodes: all nodes that are not removable or are root or have symbol in required_symbols. But note: root is also not removable. However, we must ensure that required_symbols are represented by the retained set. If initially, the required_symbols are not all present among nodes that are forced retained, then the case is infeasible (raise ValueError). But required_symbols likely are satisfied by some nodes. There might be multiple nodes with same symbol, but we only need at least one node with that symbol to remain. So we can mark nodes as mandatory if they are the only ones providing a required symbol? Actually, the problem says: \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" meaning if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, that node must be retained. So we treat any node with symbol in required_symbols as cannot be removed. That ensures that symbol remains. But if there are multiple nodes with same required symbol, they all must be retained? The description says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". It doesn't say one instance is enough; it says the node itself has a required symbol, so that node must not be removed. So all nodes with symbol in required_symbols are forced retained. That seems consistent with the fixture: \"required_symbols\": [\"root\",\"s1\"]. In the tree, there are multiple nodes with symbol s1? For example, in first case, \"n2\" has symbol s2, \"n4\" has symbol s1, \"n6\" has symbol s0. So only node with symbol s1 are n1? Actually n1 has symbol s1, and n4 also has symbol s1. Both must be retained because they have required symbol s1. And root is also required. So all nodes with symbol in required_symbols are forced retained. That is clear.\n\n3. Now we have a set of forced retained nodes. All other removable nodes are candidates for removal. We need to process candidates in order: by depth descending, then by id ascending (stable tie break). For each candidate node:\n   - Check if it is still in retained set (i.e., not already removed).\n   - Check if it has any children that are still retained. If yes, skip (cannot remove).\n   - Check if after removing this node, the set of symbols of all retained nodes still contains all required_symbols. Since we are only removing this one node, we can check if its symbol is needed. But careful: if this node's symbol is not in required_symbols, then removing it won't affect required_symbols. However, if its symbol is in required_symbols, it should have been forced retained, so this check would be redundant. But we can still check: if its symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it anyway. So we can assert that. So the only condition is that no required symbol is uniquely provided by this node? Actually, required symbols are each node with that symbol, not the symbol as a whole. So if a node has a required symbol, it cannot be removed. So we don't need to check symbol representation. But to be safe, we can check: if we remove this node, do all required symbols still have at least one node retained? But since we only remove removable nodes, and required symbol nodes are not removable, they won't be removed. So no need. However, there might be a scenario where a required symbol node is removable? In fixtures, nodes with symbol root and s1 have removable=false. So they are forced. But problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol\". So we enforce that. So we can assume forced retained nodes are not removable. So we skip the symbol representation check.\n\n   But the candidate_artifact says: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This could be interpreted as: after removal, the set of symbols among all retained nodes (including other nodes) must still contain each required symbol. Since we are not removing any node with required symbol, this is automatically true. However, if there is a node with a symbol that is not in required symbols, its removal doesn't affect.\n\n   So the only condition is that the node has no retained children.\n\n4. If the node passes, remove it: add its id to removed_node_ids list (in order of removal), and mark it as not retained. Also, update the children list of its parent? But we only need to check future decisions: when processing a parent, we need to know if any child is retained. Since we remove the child, it's no longer retained, so parent's children set decreases. So we should remove the node from the retained set and also from its parent's children list (or just check on the fly by looking up the node's retained status). So we don't need to maintain children lists dynamically; we can just check if any child id is still in retained set. That's fine.\n\n5. After processing all candidates, we have removed_node_ids in removal order. Then retained_node_ids should be sorted (alphabetically) list of all node ids that are not removed. That includes forced retained nodes and any removable nodes that were not removed.\n\n6. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained_ids}.\n\nWe also need to handle infeasible cases: If any required symbol node is not present in the nodes list? But nodes list contains all nodes. If required_symbols includes a symbol that no node has? That would be impossible to satisfy. But we can check: for each required symbol, ensure at least one node has that symbol. If not, raise ValueError. Also, root must exist and be not removable. Also ensure that the tree is consistent (parent references exist). The problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case.\" So we should validate.\n\nAlso note the output schema requires arrays of strings. So we need to sort retained_node_ids as strings.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) in Python. The case argument is a dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Possibly also \"_case_nonce\" but ignore. We'll parse nodes.\n\nEdge cases: multiple nodes with same id? Should not happen. But we can assume unique.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Build a dict node_dict = {node['id']: node}\n- Build parent-child mapping: for each node, if parent is not None, add child id to parent's children list.\n- Determine forced_retained set: all node ids where node['removable']==False or node['id']=='root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols. But note: root's removable is false anyway. We'll add all that meet condition.\n- Also ensure that required_symbols are covered: for each sym in required_sy