es:\nroot (depth0, not removable, symbol root)\nn1 (depth1, not removable, symbol s1) -> forced retained\nn2 (depth1, removable, symbol s2) -> candidate\nn3 (depth1, not removable, symbol s0) -> forced retained (not removable)\nn4 (depth2, removable, symbol s1) -> but symbol s1 is required, so forced retained (even though removable? It says removable: true but symbol s1 in required_symbols, so it must be retained. So n4 is forced retained, not candidate.\nn5 (depth2, not removable, symbol s2) -> forced retained\nn6 (depth2, removable, symbol s0) -> candidate (symbol s0 not required, removable)\n\nCandidates: n2 (depth1), n6 (depth2). Also any other? n4 is forced. So sort: depth descending: n6 (depth2) first, then n2 (depth1). Tie break id: n6 < n2? Actually n6 comes before n2 alphabetically? Compare \"n2\" and \"n6\": '2' < '6' so \"n2\" < \"n6\". So we need ascending id. So for same depth we use id ascending. But we have different depths. So order: n6 (depth2), then n2 (depth1). Now process n6: check children of n6: parent is n1. Children of n6? None? It's leaf. So no retained children. Remove n6. retained set now includes all except n6. Then process n2: children of n2: n4 (still retained because forced), so cannot remove n2 because it has retained child n4. So n2 remains. removed_ids = [\"n6\"]. retained_ids = sorted([root,n1,n2,n3,n4,n5]). Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"] (alphabetical: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root). But root starts with 'r', after 'n'? Actually 'r' > 'n'? ASCII: 'n'=110, 'r'=114, so \"n1\" < \"root\"? Compare string: \"n1\" vs \"root\": first char 'n' < 'r', so yes. So sorted list: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is that what the expected output? Not given, but we trust algorithm.\n\nCase 1 and 2 similar.\n\nWe must also handle case where no removable nodes, then removed_ids empty, retained all sorted.\n\nRaise ValueError if required symbols not satisfied.\n\nNow need to produce implementation in code.\n\nAlso 