ng. Better to avoid docstring, or use # comments. We'll keep it minimal with comments.\n\nSo the code will have newlines, which we need to encode as \\n in the JSON string. Also backslashes: if we have any (like in string escapes), we need to double them. No regex, so no backslashes.\n\nThus we can write the Python code as a string with actual newlines, then replace newline with \\n and quote the whole string. But we need to also escape any double quotes if present. Since we use single quotes, we don't need to escape double quotes, but we do need to escape single quotes inside the code? The JSON string is delimited by double quotes, so single quotes inside are fine. But if the code contains a single quote, it's okay because JSON string can contain single quotes unescaped. Only double quotes and backslash must be escaped. So we can write the code with single quotes, and then just replace newlines with \\n, and ensure no stray backslashes. However, our code will contain backslashes? Possibly in string literals like \"\\n\"? We won't use that. We'll avoid them.\n\nSo we can manually construct the JSON:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nBut we need to write the entire code with proper indentation and newlines. I'll write it as a multiline string in my response and then manually escape. Let me draft the implementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields')\n    # Build node dict\n    node_dict = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        node_dict[node['id']] = node\n    # Check required_symbols presence\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if not any(n['symbol'] == sym for n in node_dict.values()):\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present')\n    # Root check\n    if 'root' not in node_dict:\n        raise ValueError('Root node missing')\n    root = node_dict['root']\n    if root.get('removable', False):\n        raise ValueError('Root is removable')\n    # Build children map\n    children = {}\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    # Determine forced retained (cannot be removed)\n    forced_retained = set()\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\n        if not node['removable']:\n            forced_retained.add(nid)\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            forced_retained.add(nid)\n        if nid == 'root':\n            forced_retained.add(nid)\n    # All nodes start retained\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    # Candidates: removable and not forced retained\n    candidates = [node for node in node_dict.values() if node['removable'] and node['id'] not in forced_retained]\n    # Sort: deeper first (desc depth), then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed = []\n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child is retained\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\n            if child_id in retained:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\n\nWe need to be careful: children map may have missing keys for leaf nodes. Use .get.\n\nAlso, note that node['depth'] may be int. Ensure we have depth key.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON. I'll write the code with proper indentation, using 4 spaces. But when escaped, \\n and spaces will be literal. That's fine.\n\nNow, I'll manually create the JSON string. The response mus